    "start_sources": {
      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "contents": "# Boba Run Splitter\n# Totals a boba order, adds a tip, and splits it among friends.\n\nprint(\"Boba Run Splitter\")\n\nnum_drinks = int(input(\"How many drinks? \"))\ndrink_price = float(input(\"Price per drink: \"))\ncoupon = float(input(\"Coupon amount: \"))\nnum_friends = int(input(\"How many friends are splitting? \"))\n\nsubtotal = num_drinks * drink_price - coupon\ntotal_cents = round(subtotal * 1.18 * 100)\n# total_cents will be:\n\nper_person = round(total_cents / num_friends)\nleftover_cents = total_cents % num_friends\n# leftover_cents tells us:\n\nprint(\"Each person pays: $\" + str(per_person / 100))\nprint(\"Leftover cents: \" + str(leftover_cents))\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        },
        "1": {
          "id": "1",
          "name": "investigate.txt",
          "contents": "Investigate Notes — Boba Run Splitter\n\nOperator inventory\nFind one line using each of * - % / and explain the job each operator does for the user, not just for the math.\n*:\n-:\n%:\n/:\n\n1. Why is the total converted to cents before splitting? What would total / num_friends show instead?\n\n2. Why round() and not something else on total_cents?\n\n3. Rewrite the * 1.18 tip line (line 9) as two separate lines that do the same thing.\n\n4. For which per-person amounts does the output print something misleading? (Example: cents ending in 0 — 750 would print as \"$7.5\" instead of \"$7.50.\")\n\n5. What would you change about the input prompts (lines 4-7) if the user had never used a program like this before?",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
      },
